===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|bombard}}, from {{der|en|frm|bombarde||a bombard, mortar, catapult"; also "a bassoon-like musical instrument}}, from {{der|en|la|bombus||buzzing; booming}}.

The modern pronunciation is from modern {{cog|fr|bombarde}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[medieval]] primitive [[cannon]], used chiefly in [[siege]]s for throwing heavy stone balls.
#* {{RQ:Knolles Turkes
|passage=They planted in divers places twelve great '''bombards''', wherewith they threw huge stones into the air, which, falling down into the city, might break down the houses.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[bassoon]]-like medieval musical instrument.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A large liquor container made of [[leather]], in the form of a jug or a bottle.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|2|2|passage={{...}} yond same black cloud, yond huge one, / looks like a foul '''bombard''' that would shed his liquor.}}
# {{lb|en|poetic|rare}} A [[bombardment]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1807|author=w:Joel Barlow|title=The Columbiad
|passage=With mines and parallels contracts the space; <br>Then bids the battering floats his labors crown <br>And pour their bombard on the shuddering town}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[bombardon]].

