===Etymology 2===
From {{bor|en|fr|bombarder}}, from {{der|en|frm|bombarde||a bombard}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To continuously [[attack]] something with [[bomb]]s, [[artillery]] [[shell]]s or other [[missile]]s or [[projectile]]s.
#: {{ux|en|The enemy's stronghold was '''bombarded''' for 3 hours straight.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} To attack something or someone by [[directing]] objects at them.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1954|month=July|author=Cecil J. Allen|title=British Locomotive Practice and Performance|journal=Railway Magazine|page=488|text=My correspondent, who was riding in the first coach, comments that the small standard tender did not take kindly to this gay progress, and signified its disapproval from time to time by '''bombarding''' the train with lumps of coal!}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} To continuously send or direct (at someone)
#: {{ux|en|I was '''bombarded''' with WhatsApp messages after appearing on the news.}}
#: {{ux|en|Please don't '''bombard''' me with questions right now, I'll answer them at the end of the statement.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1945|author=Morton Thompson|title=Joe, the Wounded Tennis Player|page=88|passage=At this point she remembered, sitting there, surrounded by heavy breathing, the constellations flashing, cosmic rays '''bombarding''' marvelsome complex coils on the lecturer's dials, that she had forgotten to turn out the gas under the beets.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2002-12-11|passage=We're so '''bombarded''' with images, it's a struggle to preserve our imaginations.' In response, he's turned to cinema, commissioning 11 film-makers to contribute to a portmanteau film, entitled '11'09"01' and composed of short films each running 11 minutes, nine seconds and one frame.|author=Nick Bradshaw|title=One day in September|work=Time Out|page=71}}
# {{lb|en|physics}} To direct at a [[substance]] an [[intense]] [[stream]] of high-energy [[particle]]s, usually sub-atomic or made of at most a few atoms.

